"use strict";(self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara=self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara||[]).push([["67156"],{84335(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"FIN-1154","slug":"herding","term":"Herding","also_known_as":[],"difficulty":"Intermediate","definition":"Herding is the tendency of investors to follow what other investors are doing, rather than relying on their own analysis, which can push prices further and faster than fundamentals justify.","explanation":["People often feel safer doing what others do, especially when they are uncertain. In markets, this can lead investors to buy because prices are rising and others are buying, or sell because others are selling.","For example, during a speculative boom in a popular technology or asset, many investors buy simply because prices keep rising and stories of others\' gains spread, rather than because they have studied the investment. When sentiment turns, the same tendency can trigger panic selling.","Herding can contribute to bubbles and crashes, and to momentum in prices. It can affect professional investors too, since fund managers may fear underperforming their peers more than they fear being wrong together.","Not all herding is irrational; following others can make sense when they may have better information. But blindly following the crowd can lead to buying near peaks and selling near lows. Having a clear investment plan, diversifying and avoiding decisions driven by headlines or social media can help reduce the influence of herding."],"note":"","total":1500,"prev":{"slug":"hedging","term":"Hedging"},"next":{"slug":"historical-cost","term":"Historical Cost"}}')}}]);